      "id": 1,
      "text": "Marshall, Texas was founded in 1841 as the seat of Harrison County after earlier attempts failed along the Sabine River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshall%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1873 the Methodist Episcopal Church established Wiley College to educate freedmen, becoming one of the oldest historically black institutions in the U.S.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshall%2C_Texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Bishop College, founded in 1881, was another historically black college that served the African American community in Marshall.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshall%2C_Texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The railroad era began in the early 1870s, and by 1880 Marshall had become one of the South's largest cotton markets, shipping goods by rail.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshall%2C_Texas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Marshall hosts the annual Fire Ant Festival and the Wonderland of Lights, a holiday light display that has become a regional tradition.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshall%2C_Texas",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 2018 the Harrison County Historic Commission declared Marshall the birthplace of boogie woogie, honoring the city’s musical heritage.",
